  {
    "title": "Democrats Put Public Safety at Risk, Says Kauffman",
    "url": "https://www.repkauffman.com/News/38375/Press-Releases/Democrats-Put-Public-Safety-at-Risk--Says-Kauffman",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Democrats Put Public Safety at Risk, Says Kauffman\nJuly 23, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n– Legislation addressing the sentencing of convicted murders will miss the state deadline due to House Democrat maneuvers, potentially putting killers back on the streets, said Rep. Rob Kauffman (R-Franklin).\nOn March 26, the Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruled in Commonwealth of Pennsylvania v. Derek Lee, that automatic mandatory life without parole sentences for second-degree murder (also known as felony murder) are a violation of the state Constitution. The court gave the Legislature 120 days to make changes to the unconstitutional sentencing framework. That deadline is Friday, July 24. Without new measures in place addressing sentencing, hundreds of men and women already convicted of second-degree murder could be resentenced or released from incarceration.\nSecond-degree murder applies when an individual participates in certain dangerous felonies, including robbery, rape or arson, that result in another person’s death. Even if the individual did not personally cause the fatal injury, Pennsylvania law has long recognized that those who willingly participate in violent felonies that lead to a loss of life must be held accountable.\nCurrently in Pennsylvania, approximately 1,100 inmates are serving life sentences for second-degree murder.\n“Democrats avoided action on the bill until the day before the deadline. This inaction is now putting public safety at risk,” said Kauffman. “In addition, by amending the bill at this late stage, they have all but guaranteed the legislation will not be adopted by the deadline.”\nKauffman opposed the bill due to the amendments the Democrats added, which included provisions to allow expansive medical parole for inmates; and new sentencing guidelines that do not include any mandatory sentences, instead giving judges complete discretion.\n“We are talking about violent offenders, such as rapists and arsonists, who are responsible for the death of an individual or individuals,” said Kauffman. “Victims and survivors deserve better than dramatically reduced sentences, expansive medical parole and no minimum mandatory sentencing for these grievous crimes.”\nHouse Bill 1042\nnow goes back to the state Senate for consideration.\nEditor’s Note: A video clip of Rep. Rob Kauffman’s comments is available for viewing and distribution. To download the clip, follow the link, click the three dots in the lower right corner of the screen and select download.\nWatch the video here\n.\nRepresentative Rob Kauffman\n89th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717.772.9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Rob Kauffman issued a statement criticizing House Democrats for amending and delaying a bill (HB 1042) meant to address sentencing for second-degree murder convictions after a Pennsylvania Supreme Court ruling. The court struck down automatic life-without-parole sentences for second-degree murder and gave the legislature 120 days to respond, a deadline Kauffman says will be missed. Kauffman opposed the amended bill because it added medical parole provisions and removed mandatory minimum sentences, and the bill has now been sent to the state Senate.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "public_safety",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Kauffman Announces Preservation Grant for Capitol Theatre",
    "url": "https://www.repkauffman.com/News/38102/Press-Releases/Kauffman-Announces-Preservation-Grant-for-Capitol-Theatre",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Kauffman Announces Preservation Grant for Capitol Theatre\nJune 11, 2026\nHARRISBURG\n– State Rep. Rob Kauffman (R-Franklin) today announced a $30,000 grant has been awarded to the Capitol Theatre Center Foundation in Chambersburg under the Pennsylvania Historical and Museum Commission’s Keystone Historic Preservation Grant program.\n“The Capitol Theatre Center Foundation continuously works to preserve the historic building, constructed in 1927. It is now a National Historic Landmark, and is a gem in our community, offering cultural events, movies and other entertainment,” said Kauffman.\nThe funding will be used to replace water and sewer lines running beneath the theatre building. The project will ensure historic restoration of underground infrastructure from the building to the street.\nKeystone Historic Preservation Grants provide funding to support projects that identify, preserve, promote, and protect historic and archaeological resources in Pennsylvania for both the benefit of the public and the revitalization of communities. The Capitol Theatre was one of only 38 projects across the state to receive funding under the program, which requires matching funding from the recipient.\nRepresentative Rob Kauffman\n89th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717.772.9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "A $30,000 state historic preservation grant was awarded to the Capitol Theatre Center Foundation in Chambersburg, PA, to replace water and sewer lines beneath the 1927 building. The grant comes from the Pennsylvania Historical and Museum Commission's Keystone Historic Preservation Grant program. Rep. Kauffman announced the grant but did not author legislation to create it; he is publicizing funding his constituent organization received.",
      "action_type": "statement",
      "topics": [
        "infrastructure",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": true
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Franklin County Farmers Invited to Ag Breakfast Hosted by Reichard, Kauffman and Irvin",
    "url": "https://www.repkauffman.com/News/37704/Press-Releases/Franklin-County-Farmers-Invited-to-Ag-Breakfast-Hosted-by-Reichard--Kauffman-and-Irvin",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Franklin County Farmers Invited to Ag Breakfast Hosted by Reichard, Kauffman and Irvin\nMarch 18, 2026\nWAYNESBORO – Reps. Chad Reichard (R-Franklin), Rob Kauffman (R-Franklin) and Rich Irvin (R-Franklin/Huntingdon) will host an Ag Breakfast next month to bring together farmers, agricultural professionals, agribusiness leaders and residents, including those working across the broader agriculture and food systems, to discuss issues impacting Pennsylvania agriculture.\nThe free event will take place on Tuesday, April 7, from 8:30-10:30 a.m., at the Marion Volunteer Fire Company, 5465 Molly Pitcher Highway South, Chambersburg.\n“Agriculture plays a vital role in our local economy and way of life,” the three lawmakers said. “This breakfast is an opportunity for farmers and community members to connect directly with state leaders, hear the latest updates affecting the industry and share their perspectives.”\nPennsylvania Secretary of Agriculture Russell Redding is the keynote speaker and will offer updates on the state’s agriculture industry and discuss challenges and opportunities facing farmers across the Commonwealth.\n“Agriculture remains Pennsylvania’s top industry, and maintaining open communication with the people who feed and fuel our communities is essential,” the lawmakers said. “We encourage our valued farmers to join us for this informative morning.”\nThere is no cost to attend; however, RSVP is required. Residents may RSVP by calling 717-749-7384, or going online to\nRepReichard.com\n,\nRepKauffman.com\nor\nRepIrvin.com\n.\nRepresentative Chad Reichard\n90th Legislative District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Greg Gross\n717.260.6374\nggross@pahousegop.com\nRepReichard.com / Facebook.com/RepReichard\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Three Republican state representatives from Franklin County are hosting a free agricultural breakfast event on April 7, 2026 in Chambersburg. The event will feature Pennsylvania Secretary of Agriculture Russell Redding as a keynote speaker and is open to farmers, agricultural workers, and residents. Attendees must RSVP in advance by phone or through the representatives' websites.",
      "action_type": "event",
      "topics": [
        "economy",
        "other"
      ],
      "spin_flag": false
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Kauffman to Host AARP Smart Driver Courses for Older Drivers",
    "url": "https://www.repkauffman.com/News/37412/Press-Releases/Kauffman-to-Host-AARP-Smart-Driver-Courses-for-Older-Drivers",
    "date": null,
    "raw_text": "Kauffman to Host AARP Smart Driver Courses for Older Drivers\nFebruary 2, 2026\nAttendees eligible for discount on auto insurance premiums\nHARRISBURG – Rep. Rob Kauffman (R-Franklin) is once again hosting the AARP Smart Driver Course for older drivers living in the 89th District in Franklin County, which includes the townships of Greene, Guilford and Hamilton, and Chambersburg Borough.\n“This is a popular course that offers older motorists tips and techniques to help them become more confident and safer drivers,” said Kauffman. “I am hosting both a refresher class, as well as the full two-day course for those who are interested. Registration will be on a first-come, first-served basis.”\nThe AARP Smart Driver Course teaches participants the current rules of the road, defensive driving techniques and how to operate their vehicles more safely in today’s increasingly challenging driving environment.\nAll classes will be held from 9 a.m. to 1 p.m. at Menno Haven Life Center, 300 Ridgeview in Chambersburg. Dates are as follows:\n•\tApril 20 - This is a refresher class for anyone who attended in 2023. The refresher class is recommended every three years.\n•\tJune 16 and 17 – Must attend both classes.\n•\tAug. 17 and 18 – Must attend both classes.\nThere are no tests to pass. The course costs $20 for AARP members and $25 for non-members paid by check or money order. Space is limited. Register by calling Kauffman’s district office at 717-264-3943.\nUpon completion of the course, participants may be eligible to receive Pennsylvania’s multi-year auto insurance discount for insured drivers age 55 and older (consult your insurance agent for details).\nRepresentative Rob Kauffman\n89th District\nPennsylvania House of Representatives\nMedia Contact: Tricia Lehman\n717.772.9840\ntlehman@pahousegop.com\nRepKauffman.com\nShare\nSign Up to Receive Legislative Email Updates\nKeep up-to-date on the latest legislative and community news. Your email address will be used strictly for legislative purposes.\nEmail*\nZIP Code\nSubscribe",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "Rep. Rob Kauffman is organizing AARP Smart Driver Courses for older residents in his Franklin County district. The classes will be held at Menno Haven Life Center in Chambersburg on several dates in April, June, and August 2026, covering defensive driving skills and updated road rules. The course costs $20-$25 and completing it may qualify participants aged 55 and older for an auto insurance discount.",
